# The `@extend` Directive 🔗

The `@extend` directive tells Sass that one selector should inherit the styles of another selector.

```scss_not
// Base alert message
.message {
  padding: 12px 16px;
  border-radius: 6px;
  font-size: 14px;
  border: 1px solid #cbd5e1;
}

// Specific error alert extending .message
.message-error {
@extend .message;
  background-color: #fef2f2;
  border-color: #f87171;
  color: #b91c1c;
}
```

## How Sass Compiles `@extend`

Instead of copying properties, Sass groups the selectors together in the compiled CSS:

```css_not
.message, .message-error {
  padding: 12px 16px;
  border-radius: 6px;
  font-size: 14px;
  border: 1px solid #cbd5e1;
}

.message-error {
  background-color: #fef2f2;
  border-color: #f87171;
  color: #b91c1c;
}
```

# Placeholder Selectors: `%` 👻

In the previous example, `.message` was compiled into the CSS even if no HTML element used the generic class. **Placeholder Selectors (`%`)** solve this!

## What is a Placeholder?

A placeholder begins with `%` instead of `.` or `#`. It is completely invisible to compiled CSS **unless extended**:

```scss_not
// Ghost placeholder: Won't appear in CSS output on its own!
%flex-center {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}

.badge {
  @extend %flex-center;
  padding: 4px 8px;
}

.modal-header {
  @extend %flex-center;
  padding: 16px;
}
```

## Compiles To:
```css_not
.badge, .modal-header {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
.badge { padding: 4px 8px; }
.modal-header { padding: 16px; }
```

# Multiple Extends & Inheritance Chaining ⛓️

A selector can extend multiple placeholders or classes, forming powerful inheritance chains.

```scss_not
%reset-btn {
  border: none;
  background: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  font: inherit;
}

%pill-shape {
  border-radius: 9999px;
  padding: 8px 24px;
}

.pill-button {
  @extend %reset-btn;
  @extend %pill-shape;
  background-color: #6366f1;
  color: white;
}

// Chained extension:
.pill-button-danger {
  @extend .pill-button; // Inherits %reset-btn, %pill-shape, AND .pill-button styles!
  background-color: #ef4444;
}
```

# The Great `@extend` Media Query Limitation 🛑

Sass does **NOT** allow a selector inside an `@media` query to extend a selector defined outside that query!

## The Breaking Scenario ❌

```scss_not
%pill-badge {
  border-radius: 9999px;
  padding: 4px 8px;
}

@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .badge {
    // COMPILATION ERROR!
    // You may not @extend an outer selector from within @media.
    @extend %pill-badge;
  }
}
```

## The Solution ✅

Use a `@mixin` instead! Mixins can be freely included inside media queries without restrictions.

# Preventing Crashes with `!optional` 🛡️

By default, if you `@extend` a selector that doesn't exist in your stylesheet, Sass throws a compilation error.

```scss_not
// If .custom-theme is missing or commented out, this crashes the build!
.widget {
  @extend .custom-theme;
}
```

## Using `!optional`

Add `!optional` to tell the compiler: *"Extend if available, but don't fail if the selector is not found."*

```scss_not
.widget {
  @extend .custom-theme !optional;
}
```

# Arithmetic Operators: `*` and `math.div()` ✖️➗

## Multiplication (`*`)

When multiplying, only **one** operand can have a unit (multiplying two units creates squared units like `px*px` which is invalid CSS):

```scss_not
$spacing: 8px;
.container {
  padding: $spacing * 3; // 24px (CORRECT)
  // padding: 8px * 3px; // ERROR: 24px*px is invalid in CSS!
}
```

## Modern Division (`math.div`)

```scss_not
@use 'sass:math';

$grid-cols: 12;
.col-4 {
  width: math.div(4, $grid-cols) * 100%; // 33.3333333333%
}
```

# The Modulo (Remainder) Operator: `%` ➗

The `%` operator computes the integer remainder after division.

```scss_not
@use 'sass:math';

$remainder: 14 % 4; // 2 (since 14 = 3 * 4 + 2)

// Alternating pattern check
@for $i from 1 through 6 {
  .item-#{$i} {
    @if $i % 2 == 0 {
      background-color: #f8fafc;
    } @else {
      background-color: #ffffff;
    }
  }
}
```

# Comparison & Equality Operators 🔍

Sass supports all standard comparison operators for numbers, strings, and colors.

## Equality Operators

- `==` (Equal to): `5 == 5` (true), `'sans-serif' == sans-serif` (true — string quotes are ignored in comparison!)
- `!=` (Not equal to): `red != blue` (true)

## Relational Operators (Numbers)

- `<` (Less than), `<=` (Less than or equal)
- `>` (Greater than), `>=` (Greater than or equal)

```scss_not
$font-size: 18px;

h2 {
  @if $font-size >= 18px {
    line-height: 1.4;
  } @else {
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
}
```

# Logical Operators: `and`, `or`, `not` 🔀

Combine boolean conditions using human-readable words instead of `&&`, `||`, and `!`.

```scss_not
$theme: 'dark';
$high-contrast: true;

.btn {
  // Logical 'and'
  @if $theme == 'dark' and $high-contrast {
    border: 2px solid white;
    background: black;
  }

  // Logical 'or'
  @if $theme == 'dark' or $theme == 'cyberpunk' {
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);
  }

  // Logical 'not'
  @if not $high-contrast {
    opacity: 0.9;
  }
}
```

# String Operators & String Joining 🔤

Combine strings using the plus `+` operator.

```scss_not
$font-name: 'Inter';
$fallback: ', sans-serif';
$font-stack: $font-name + $fallback; // 'Inter, sans-serif'

// Quoted + Unquoted strings:
// The result takes the quotation status of the LEFT operand!
$test1: 'Hello ' + World; // 'Hello World' (Quoted)
$test2: Hello + ' World'; // Hello World   (Unquoted)

.element {
  content: 'Path: ' + '/images/avatar.png';
}
```

# Escaping Selectors with `@at-root` 🌳

The `@at-root` directive causes rules nested inside a block to be emitted at the root of the document instead of nested under the parent.

```scss_not
.parent-card {
  background: white;
  padding: 16px;

  // Render modal-overlay at root level without .parent-card prefix
  @at-root .modal-overlay {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
  }
}
```

## Compiles To:
```css_not
.parent-card { background: white; padding: 16px; }
.modal-overlay { position: fixed; inset: 0; background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6); }
```

# Production Pattern: The Polymorphic Alert Suite 🚨

Combining `%` placeholders, `@extend`, and math operators:

```scss_not
%base-alert {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
  border-radius: 0.5rem;
  border: 1px solid transparent;
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  line-height: 1.5;
}

.alert-info {
  @extend %base-alert;
  background-color: #eff6ff;
  border-color: #bfdbfe;
  color: #1e40af;
}

.alert-success {
  @extend %base-alert;
  background-color: #f0fdf4;
  border-color: #bbf7d0;
  color: #166534;
}

.alert-warning {
  @extend %base-alert;
  background-color: #fffbeb;
  border-color: #fde68a;
  color: #92400e;
}
```

# The Selector Explosion Antipattern 💥

If you use `@extend` indiscriminately on deeply nested classes, Sass creates a combinatorial explosion of selectors:

```scss_not
// If .a is extended by .b, and .c .d extends .e...
// Sass compiles every permutation:
.a, .b, .c .d, .c .a, .b .d { ... }
```

## How to Avoid It:
Only `@extend` standalone placeholder selectors (`%name`). Never `@extend` complex descendant selectors like `.card .body p`!
